artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the Einstein–Yang–Mills (EYM) system on a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold (M,g) with an SU(2) connection A and curvature F = dA + A∧A. The equations are

  Ric(g) − (1/2) R(g) g = 8π T(F,g),   d_A ⋆ F = 0,

where T(F,g) is the Yang–Mills stress–energy tensor T_{μν} = (1/4π)( F_{μα} F_ν{}^α − (1/4) g_{μν} F_{αβ} F^{αβ} ).

Assume spherical symmetry: M admits an SO(3) action by isometries whose generic orbits are 2-spheres, and the metric takes the form

  g = −α(t,r)² dt² + a(t,r)² dr² + r² (dθ² + sin²θ dφ²),

with an SO(3)-invariant SU(2) Yang–Mills connection (parametrized in the standard way by a single real "magnetic" function w(t,r) and possibly an "electric" function, both depending on t and r).

Develop a rigorous mathematical analysis of the resulting time-dependent spherically symmetric EYM equations as a Cauchy problem (or characteristic initial value problem) for the unknowns (α, a, w, …), in analogy with the well-developed analysis of the spherically symmetric Einstein-scalar field system (which includes results on local and global well-posedness, formation of trapped surfaces, weak cosmic censorship, critical collapse, and the structure of singularities). In particular, establish results on:

(i) local existence, uniqueness, and continuation criteria for solutions arising from regular asymptotically flat initial data;
(ii) global behavior, including conditions for global regularity versus gravitational collapse and black hole formation;
(iii) the asymptotic structure (decay, completeness of null infinity) of global-in-time solutions;
(iv) weak and strong cosmic censorship within this symmetry class;
(v) critical phenomena in the threshold of black hole formation.
